  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Monday: Expect warmer than normal temperatures through \nthe rest of the weekend and into next week as the upper level \nridge over the Pacific Northwest stays in place. Skies will be \nmostly clear through Monday with no precipitation expected \nacross the forecast area. Sunday and Monday are expected to be \nthe warmest days, with high temperatures in the low to mid 80s, \nroughly 10 degrees warmer than normal for this time of year. \n\nTuesday through Saturday: On Tuesday morning, a low will drop \nsoutheast off the British Columbia coast, and lower heights will \nbrush across the state of Washington through the end of the work \nweek. Cluster analysis shows a 90 percent chance that the low will \nremain far enough off the PNW coast that little to no precipitation \nis expected. However, it will result in temperatures dropping\ninto low 70s by Wednesday, and cloud cover increasing through \nthe week. On Thursday night into Friday, long- range guidance \nfavors the low retrograding southwestward into the Pacific and \nthen moving northeastward. Cluster analysis is divided on the \ntrack of the low; 60 percent of the clusters show the low close\nenough to result in chances for light precipitation across the \narea Friday into the weekend. The other 40 percent show the area\nremaining dry. For right now, the bottom line is: expect \noverall dry conditions and temperatures cooling down to normal \nby midweek. /AS",
